Spec: the identity implementation MUST reject an X25519 stanza with other than exactly two arguments, or whose second argument is not a canonical base64 encoding of a 32-byte value.

Header::parse splits the arg line on whitespace and passes whatever it finds through to Stanza::X25519::unwrap. The 32-byte body-length check the spec also requires (against partitioning oracles) IS effectively enforced via unwrap_file_key croaking, but it is swallowed by the eval in unwrap.
Implemented, uncommitted in the working tree (maintainer reviews and commits).

Measured on HEAD first, all six malformed cases got past Header::parse:
  - two arguments: unwrap_file_key returned the correct file key. Accepted in
    silence -- the extra argument was simply ignored.
  - no argument: died in CryptX with 'FATAL: undefined key' after six 'Use of
    uninitialized value' warnings from the base64 decoder.
  - argument not 32 bytes: died in CryptX with 'FATAL: invalid key'. Incidental
    -- that is import_key_raw's own length check, not an age-format check.
  - body not 32 bytes: ticket claim CONFIRMED. Primitives::unwrap_file_key
    croaks, the eval in Stanza::X25519::unwrap swallows it, unwrap returns
    undef, and Header::unwrap_file_key reports 'No matching identity found' --
    a soft no-match where the spec requires a header failure.

Fix: a BUILD in Crypt::Age::Stanza::X25519 enforcing exactly one argument after
the type, an argument decoding to 32 bytes, and a 32-byte body. Chosen over
Header::parse or unwrap because Header::parse already dispatches on the type
string to pick this class, so the check fires at parse time (header failure,
before any identity is consulted) and is scoped to X25519 by class dispatch --
unknown types stay plain Crypt::Age::Stanza objects and are still ignored, so
grease needs no exemption. body is 'ro', so the 32-byte invariant holds for the
object's lifetime; that is what leaves the eval in unwrap swallowing only the
AEAD authentication failure, i.e. the genuine wrong-identity case. Base64
canonicality is not re-checked -- the strict decoder from karr #3/#4 already
covers padding, alphabet, impossible length and non-canonical trailing bits.

Error messages name the reason only; tests assert the argument and body never
appear in the error text.

